.Dd October 23, 2026
.Dt INSTALLER 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm installer
.Nd install OxideBSD from the install medium
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /usr/libexec/installer
.Op Fl t Ar tty
.Nm /usr/libexec/installer
.Fl f Ar installerconfig
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs on the console of a machine booted from the install medium,
.Pa install.iso ,
written to a CD or, with
.Xr dd 1 ,
to a USB stick.
The medium boots on BIOS and UEFI into its mfsroot: a small root file system the
boot loader loads into memory beside the kernel, which the kernel mounts as
.Pa /
when its command line says
.Li mfsroot .
Nothing written to it lasts past the boot.
The mfsroot's
.Pa /etc/ttys
runs
.Nm
on the console in place of
.Xr getty 8 ,
so
.Xr init 8
starts it again whenever it exits.
.Pp
Its first screen offers:
.Bl -tag -width Install
.It Install
Install OxideBSD on a disk of this machine.
Not yet written: the install steps' screens come next; until then, install with
.Fl f
from the shell.
.It Shell
A shell on the mfsroot, for recovery:
.Xr rescue 8 Ns 's
tools, with the machine's disks in
.Pa /dev
and their file systems mountable;
.Ic exit
returns to the first screen.
.It Reboot
Restarts the machine.
.El
.Pp
The arrow keys, or
.Ic j
and
.Ic k ,
move between them; Enter chooses.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar installerconfig
Install with no screens, every answer read from
.Ar installerconfig
(below), and exit 0 once the new system is in place, telling each step on the
standard output.
A disk it names with a new table, or with
.Li WHOLE ,
is erased without asking.
.It Fl t Ar tty
Take
.Pa /dev/ Ns Ar tty
as the controlling terminal of a session of its own, and as the standard input,
output and error, as
.Xr getty 8
does.
.El
.Sh INSTALLING
An install, from the screens or from an
.Ar installerconfig :
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
finds the install medium among the CD drives by its volume label,
.Li OXIDEBSD_INSTALL ,
mounts it read-only on
.Pa /dist ,
and checks each chosen set against its
.Pa MANIFEST ;
.It
writes the disk's new partition table, or adds OxideBSD's partitions to the
largest free space of the table it has, every other partition kept; writes
Limine's BIOS boot code; and makes each new partition's file system:
.Xr newfs_oxfs 8
and, on a new EFI system partition, FAT32
.Pq Xr newfs_msdos 8 ;
an EFI system partition the disk has already is used as it is;
.It
mounts them under
.Pa /mnt
and extracts the sets onto them;
.It
writes the new system's
.Xr fstab 5
(each partition by its GPT GUID,
.Pa /dev/gptid/ Ns Ar guid ,
which no other disk shares),
its host name in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
its keyboard layout in
.Xr wsconsctl.conf 5 ,
and root's password;
.It
puts Limine
.Po Pa EFI/OxideBSD/BOOTX64.EFI , Pa boot/limine/limine-bios.sys ,
and
.Pa EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI
if no other system has it
.Pc ,
the kernel and its
.Pa fsconf
.Pq Xr fsconf_mkdb 8
in
.Pa EFI/OxideBSD/ Ns Ar id ,
on the EFI system partition, so the disk boots on BIOS and on UEFI;
.Ar id
is the first 8 digits of the root's GUID;
.It
adds the new system to
.Pa boot/limine/limine.conf
there (a new one, or the one another system left, nothing in it removed),
with an entry for each other system's boot loader found on the machine's
EFI system partitions (Windows, shim or GRUB, systemd-boot, another Limine,
any other
.Pa EFI/ Ns Ar dir Ns Pa /*.efi )
and, for BIOS, each MBR disk's active partitions and each FreeBSD boot
partition;
.It
on UEFI, adds a firmware boot entry,
.Li OxideBSD ,
first in the boot order
.Pq Xr efibootmgr 8 ;
.It
runs the
.Ar installerconfig Ns 's
script, if it has one, in the new system
.Pq Xr chroot 2 ,
with the EFI system partition on
.Pa /boot/efi ;
.It
unmounts everything.
.El
.Sh INSTALLERCONFIG
The answer file is FreeBSD's
.Xr bsdinstall 8
format: a preamble of shell variable assignments, run by
.Xr sh 1 ,
then optionally a line
.Li #!/bin/sh
and a script for the new system.
The variables are:
.Bl -tag -width DISTRIBUTIONS
.It Ev PARTITIONS
.Li DEFAULT
(or unset): the guided layout on the first disk that isn't a CD drive.
.Oo Ar disk Oc Op Li BESIDE | Li WHOLE :
the guided layout on
.Ar disk
(or the first):
.Li WHOLE
over the whole disk, erasing it;
.Li BESIDE
in the largest free space of its partition table, every partition there
kept; neither word means
.Li WHOLE
on a disk with no partition table and
.Li BESIDE
on one with.
Otherwise
.Ar disk Oo Li GPT | Li MBR Oc Li { Ar size type Oo Ar mountpoint Oc , ... Li } :
a new table of those partitions, in order;
.Ar size
in bytes or with
.Li K ,
.Li M ,
.Li G
or
.Li T ,
or
.Li auto
for what's left (one partition at most);
.Ar type
one of
.Li oxfs ,
.Li efi ,
.Li bios
(a BIOS boot partition) and
.Li swap ;
an
.Li oxfs
partition is mounted on its
.Ar mountpoint ,
and one of them must be
.Pa / .
The guided layout is a GPT of a 512 KiB BIOS boot partition, a 260 MiB EFI
system partition, and
.Pa /
over the rest; beside other systems, a BIOS boot partition of its own (GPT),
an EFI system partition only if the disk has none, and
.Pa /
over the rest of the free space, at least 1 GiB.
.It Ev DISTRIBUTIONS
The sets, by file name:
.Li base.tar.zst
is always among them; also
.Li clang.tar.zst
and
.Li tests.tar.zst .
.It Ev HOSTNAME
The new system's host name.
.It Ev KEYMAP
Its keyboard layout, a
.Xr wsconsctl 8
.Li keyboard.encoding
name.
.It Ev ROOTPASS
Root's password.
.El
.Pp
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PARTITIONS="ide0 GPT { 512K bios, 260M efi, 20G oxfs /, auto oxfs /home }"
DISTRIBUTIONS="base.tar.zst clang.tar.zst"
HOSTNAME=oxide
KEYMAP=de
#!/bin/sh
pw useradd alice -m -G wheel
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /OxideBSD/<version>/MANIFEST -compact
.It Pa /boot/mfsroot
the mfsroot on the medium, compressed
.It Pa /OxideBSD/ Ns Ar version Ns Pa /*.tar.zst
the sets
.It Pa /OxideBSD/ Ns Ar version Ns Pa /MANIFEST
each set's SHA-256 and number of files
.It Pa /boot/limine/limine-bios-hdd.bin
Limine's BIOS boot code, for the new disk
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr ttys 5 ,
.Xr efibootmgr 8 ,
.Xr gpart 8 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr rescue 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in OxideBSD 0.3.
